Boeing will temporarily stop making its  troubled 737 max jet next month a  decision that could have a big impact on  the US economy tens of thousands of jobs  are linked to the airliners production  the 737 max as you may recall was  grounded in March after software issues  were tied to two deadly crashes that  killed 346 people Kris Van Cleave is in  Washington with more on this story good  morning to you Chris has caught a lot of  people by surprise so what does this  mean for workers now well good morning  for now the 12,000 Boeing employees who  work on the 737 will not face layoffs or  furloughs but boeing has 600 suppliers  so this move could impact tens of  thousands of supply chain jobs across  the country it's been 22 years since  boeing has had to temporarily halt  production of an airplane the 737 max is  estimated to account for about one third  of Boeing's revenue over the next five  years for months the company has said if  the max grounding was prolonged or  stretched into 2020 they'd have to  consider cutting production back in  April Boeing slashed output by 20  percent but they've built about 400 of  these 737s they cannot deliver to  Airlines until the FAA certifies the  plane as safe CEO Dennis Muhlenberg  tweeted we're keeping our employees  customers and supply chain top of mind  as we prioritize safely returning the  max to service the FAA is currently in  the process of reviewing Boeing's design  changes to the airplane certification is  possible sometimes in it sometime next  year and just this morning Southwest  Airlines joined American in removing the  Macs from its flight schedule until at  least April Tony we do hope people keep  their jobs Chris thank you very much now  a frightening malfunction forced an  emergency landing for a United Airlines  flight video from a passenger shows what  looks like flames shooting out of an  engine on United flight 366 the Boeing  737 left San Diego Sunday night it was  bound for Chicago but it only made it as  far as Albuquerque United is calling it  a mechanical issue clearly it is yeah  what you don't want to see 30,000 plus  feet in the air that's very very scary  I'd say
 

